\begin{abstract}
Action recognition models often rely on static spatial content, such as background scenes and subject appearance, instead of motion. Background bias has received attention, but foreground bias remains underexplored, and evaluation protocols may leak background cues. We propose ALBAR, a simple end-to-end adversarial training method that discourages reliance on static cues without auxiliary labels or critic networks. Each motion clip is paired with a static clip built by stacking one of its frames; a shared encoder is trained with cross-entropy on the motion clip, a negative cross-entropy (adversarial) loss and an entropy-maximization loss on the static clip, and a gradient penalty on the static input. We also show that the widely used UCF101 SCUBA/SCUFO protocol leaks background through bounding-box masks, and release an improved protocol with tight segmentation masks. With a Swin-T backbone pretrained on Kinetics-400, ALBAR raises contrasted accuracy on HMDB51 from 27.84\% to 53.22\%, 12.9 points above the previous best (StillMix, 40.28\%), and from 17.53\% to 26.23\% on the improved UCF101 protocol. It also yields gains on downstream anomaly detection and temporal action localization.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Video action recognition models frequently exploit spurious correlations with static content instead of motion \citep{choi2019can,li2018resound,li2019repair,li2023mitigating}. Two failure modes follow: \emph{background bias}, e.g., predicting ``Playing Soccer'' on a soccer field regardless of the motion, and \emph{foreground bias}, i.e., predicting from the attire, skin tone or other static appearance of the subject. Background bias has received some attention, whereas foreground bias remains underexplored despite its implications for safety and fairness in surveillance, assisted living and autonomous systems.

Existing debiasing approaches require extra supervision such as scene or object labels or attribute classifiers, rely on complex pipelines such as optical flow or decoupled spatial and temporal encoders, or use augmentations that mainly target background bias \citep{choi2019can,wang2021removing,ding2022motion,li2023mitigating}. Moreover, evaluation protocols can contain shortcuts: the SCUBA/SCUFO protocol of \citet{li2023mitigating} on UCF101 builds actor masks from bounding boxes, which let background leak around the actor and can inflate perceived robustness.

We address both issues. Our key insight is that if a model cannot confidently predict the action from a motionless clip, it must rely on motion to succeed. We therefore build an adversarial loop with a single 3D encoder: it is trained to predict correctly on normal clips while being discouraged from confident predictions on the corresponding static clips. To avoid the trivial solution of being confidently wrong on static clips, we maximize the entropy of static predictions, and we stabilize training with a gradient penalty on the static inputs.

Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{ALBAR}, a simple end-to-end adversarial framework requiring no auxiliary attribute labels or critic networks, which jointly discourages static-cue reliance (negative cross-entropy), maximizes static uncertainty (entropy) and stabilizes training (gradient penalty).
\item A study of foreground and background biases with state-of-the-art results on SCUBA/SCUFO, including $+12.9$ points of contrasted accuracy over the prior best on HMDB51.
\item The identification and correction of background leakage in the UCF101 SCUBA/SCUFO protocol through tight actor segmentation masks, and a public release of the improved protocol.
\item Evidence of downstream benefits on anomaly detection and temporal action localization.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Works}
\paragraph{Static bias in action recognition.} Action datasets contain strong scene and object correlations \citep{li2018resound,li2019repair,byvshev20223d}. Prior work mitigates background bias with an adversarial scene classifier (SDN) \citep{choi2019can}, by adding backgrounds to videos \citep{wang2021removing}, by foreground--background merging \citep{ding2022motion}, with resampling \citep{li2019repair}, or with scene-debiased open-set recognition \citep{zhai2023soar}. Data augmentation methods such as Mixup \citep{zhang2017mixup} and VideoMix \citep{yun2020videomix}, and actor-based mixing \citep{zou2023learning}, add invariances. StillMix \citep{li2023mitigating} mixes in static frames from a bias-only model and introduced the SCUBA/SCUFO protocols for background and foreground bias. In contrast, ALBAR needs no bias-model or extra labels and directly penalizes the static prediction of the same encoder.

\paragraph{Adversarial debiasing.} Adversarial learning has been used to remove protected or nuisance attributes from representations \citep{zhang2018mitigating,xie2017controllable,elazar2018adversarial,beutel2017data,wu2020privacy}, typically with a separate critic or attribute labels. We instead use the main encoder itself to produce the adversarial signal from a static version of the input, and add entropy maximization and a gradient penalty \citep{gulrajani2017improved} for stability.

\paragraph{Bias evaluation.} SCUBA replaces the background of a test video, and SCUFO stacks a single frame to remove motion \citep{li2023mitigating}. We keep this design but fix the mask quality using segmentation and tracking \citep{cheng2023segment,kirillov2023segment,yang2021aot,yang2022deaot}.

\section{Method}
\subsection{Overview}
ALBAR pairs each motion clip with a matched static clip created from a single frame. The same encoder processes both (Figure~\ref{fig:arch}). The objective combines a supervised cross-entropy on the motion clip, an adversarial negative cross-entropy on the static clip, entropy maximization on the static prediction, and a gradient penalty on the static input.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/main_architecture.png}
\caption{Overview of ALBAR. Given video clip $\mathbf{x}^{(i)}_{t}$, we sample a frame and stack it to create the static clip $\mathbf{\bar{x}}^{(i)}_{\bar{t}}$. Both clips are processed by the same encoder.}
\label{fig:arch}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Static adversarial loss}
Given a clip $x^{(i)}_t$ with label $y^{(i)}$, we sample a frame at time $\bar t$ and stack it $F$ times to form a static clip $\bar x^{(i)}_{\bar t}$. With the shared encoder $F(\cdot)$ we compute $p^{(i)}_t=F(x^{(i)}_t)$ and $\bar p^{(i)}_{\bar t}=F(\bar x^{(i)}_{\bar t})$. The supervised loss on the motion clip is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}^{(i)}_{CE} = -\sum_{c=1}^{N_C} y^{(i)}_c \log p^{(i)}_c .
\end{equation}
The adversarial loss maximizes the cross-entropy on the static clip (implemented through gradient reversal):
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}^{(i)}_{adv} = -\mathcal{L}_{CE}\big(\bar p^{(i)}_{\bar t}, y^{(i)}\big).
\end{equation}
This pushes the model not to align static predictions with labels, forcing reliance on motion.

\subsection{Entropy maximization on static clips}
Without a constraint the model may learn to be confidently wrong on static inputs. To avoid this we maximize the entropy of $\bar p$:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}^{(i)}_{ent} = \sum_c \bar p^{(i)}_{\bar t,c}\log \bar p^{(i)}_{\bar t,c}.
\end{equation}
Minimizing $\mathcal{L}_{ent}$ maximizes the entropy $H(p)=-\sum_c p_c\log p_c$, encouraging uniform predictions on static clips.

\subsection{Gradient penalty on static inputs}
Adversarial signals can make training unstable. We regularize the sensitivity of the model to static inputs:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}^{(i)}_{gp} = \big\|\nabla_{\bar x^{(i)}_{\bar t}} F(\bar x^{(i)}_{\bar t})\big\|_2 .
\end{equation}

\subsection{Combined objective}
The overall per-sample loss is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}^{(i)} = \mathcal{L}^{(i)}_{CE} + \omega_{adv}\mathcal{L}^{(i)}_{adv} + \omega_{ent}\mathcal{L}^{(i)}_{ent} + \omega_{gp}\mathcal{L}^{(i)}_{gp}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Improved UCF101 SCUBA/SCUFO protocol}
SCUBA/SCUFO evaluate background and foreground bias \citep{li2023mitigating}. SCUBA replaces the background of UCF101 test videos using Places365 images \citep{zhou2017places}, VQGAN-CLIP generated images \citep{crowson2022vqgan}, or sinusoidal stripe patterns, preserving the actor through a mask. SCUFO stacks a single frame of a SCUBA video to obtain a static foreground-only clip. The original UCF101 protocol uses THUMOS-14 \citep{THUMOS14} bounding boxes as masks, which let background leak (e.g., snow around a skier, Figure~\ref{fig:scuba}a). We instead initialize from the THUMOS-14 boxes and segment and track the actor with SAMTrack \citep{cheng2023segment} (built on SAM, AOT and DeAOT), obtaining tight actor masks (Figure~\ref{fig:scuba}b). Masks are generated automatically and then manually checked for every test video so that no residual background remains. No external attribute labels are required, and splits and class counts are inherited from the original design.

