\section{Limitations}
Our evaluation targets scene- and appearance-induced static biases; broader demographic and real-world fairness dimensions remain unexplored. The assumption that static information is uniformly unhelpful may be too strict for actions where static cues are legitimately discriminative; future work could retain beneficial spatial cues while suppressing spurious ones. ALBAR also slightly lowers IID accuracy on HMDB51. Finally, static-frame selection could use learned indicators such as action saliency to create stronger adversarial negatives without heavy inductive bias or external detectors.

\section{Conclusion}
We presented ALBAR, a label-free, single-encoder adversarial framework that breaks the correlation between labels and static cues by combining negative cross-entropy, entropy maximization and a gradient penalty on static clips. Together with a corrected UCF101 SCUBA/SCUFO protocol free of background leakage, ALBAR sets new results on bias benchmarks (53.22\% contrasted accuracy on HMDB51, $+12.9$ points over the prior best) and benefits downstream tasks, supporting more robust, motion-based video understanding.

